  randomised directory whose path is recorded in a pointer file under the tests
  directory; the suites read that pointer to locate bin/ and assets/
  subdirectories. The fixtures are copies of a fake gRPC backend binary and are
  laid down at run time, not pre-authored.
  The probe receives a -cwd=<dir> flag and chdirs there before performing any
  load; the model loader uses /tmp/probe/models as its model path.
  The runner sets CGO_ENABLED=1 and GOFLAGS=-mod=mod for the build.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side — not the implementation — consists of: a pytest runner that
  invokes a compiled probe binary over a process boundary with -flag=value
  arguments; the probe sources and binary (owned by the tests directory) that
  import and link the project package; a fake gRPC backend binary (owned by the
  tests directory) that the loader spawns and that answers the gRPC round trip;
  the runner script that compiles the probe, restores the generated protobuf,
  stages the fixture binaries at randomised paths, and runs the suites; and the
  Python probe client that drives the probe and reads the JSON it prints on
  stdout. The probe drives the loader and reports whether the load completed and
  whether a backend process was spawned and is alive, as the delivery proof that
  the route was exercised end to end. None of these — the probe, the fake backend,
  the runner, the client, or the delivery-check — is part of the implementation;
  the engineer does not rebuild them.
