## Reached at
  A compiled Go CLI binary, invoked by absolute path:

      /usr/local/bin/syft <subcommand> [flags] [source]

  The binary is the build artifact of the Go module `github.com/anchore/syft` checked
  out at `/app` (upstream tag v0.69.1, staged by the image build with its git history
  removed). The image builds it with:

      go build -o /usr/local/bin/syft ./cmd/syft/

  The entrypoint package is `/app/cmd/syft` (package main calls `cli.New()` in package
  `cmd/syft/cli`, which registers the cobra command tree). Callers execute the binary
  as a child process with working directory `/tmp` and a fully constructed environment.
  Every invocation from both caller suites goes through this exact path — no caller
  resolves `syft` from `PATH`, wraps it, or uses any other entrypoint — so every byte
  the callers observe on the child'"'"'s stdout and stderr flows through code built from
  `/app`.

  Two surfaces of the child process are read by the callers:
    - the scan report, written to the child'"'"'s stdout; in the `json` and `syft-json`
      formats (`json` is an alias of `syft-json`) the report document carries a
      `descriptor` object whose `configuration` member is the application
      configuration value serialized with its JSON tags (format code under
      `syft/formats/syftjson`);
    - the logging stream, written to the child'"'"'s stderr; at verbosity >= 2 the
      `packages` command emits a dump of the application configuration there before
      the scan runs (emitted by the CLI layer in `cmd/syft/cli` at debug log level;
      the dumped object is `internal/config.Application` rendered as YAML, and the
      dump covers the configuration sections including the `attest` section).

## Must provide
  /usr/local/bin/syft        executable build artifact of the `/app` module (package
                             `./cmd/syft/`); callers check this path exists before
                             invoking and never run any other syft
  packages                   subcommand; takes one positional SOURCE argument
  version                    subcommand; invoked with no positional argument
  attest                     subcommand registered on the root command; callers read
                             the root `--help` output, which names it, without
                             invoking it
  -v / -vv / -vvv            persistent count flag (`--verbose`) on the commands;
                             callers invoke it with one, two, and three occurrences
  -o <format>                report-format flag on `packages`; callers pass `json`,
                             `table`, `text`, and `syft-json`
  SYFT_* environment binding configuration keys are populated from `SYFT_`-prefixed
                             environment variables (application-name prefix, with
                             `.` and `-` mapped to `_`); callers drive
                             `SYFT_CHECK_FOR_APP_UPDATE`, `SYFT_OUTPUT`,
                             `SYFT_PARALLELISM`, `SYFT_ATTEST_PASSWORD`,
                             `SYFT_ATTEST_KEY` through this binding
  config dump on stderr      at verbosity >= 2 the application-config dump is emitted
                             on the child'"'"'s stderr, covering the configuration
                             including the attest section
  descriptor.configuration   the `json` / `syft-json` report on stdout carries the
                             application configuration under `descriptor` ->
                             `configuration`

## Called as
  Every invocation is a child-process execution of the absolute path above, with a
  caller-built environment dict, cwd `/tmp`, and stdout and stderr captured
  separately.

  syft version               no positional argument
  syft --help                no positional argument
  syft packages [-v|-vv|-vvv] [-o <format>] <source>
      <source>   1st positional argument; a string naming a path on the local
                 filesystem — callers pass paths to single files and paths to
                 directories
      -o         flag value; a string naming a report format
      -v/-vv/-vv flag occurrences; an occurrence count, callers use 1, 2, and 3
  The child environment always carries `PATH`, `HOME`, and `SYFT_CHECK_FOR_APP_UPDATE`
  (each a string). Depending on the invocation, callers additionally set any of:
      SYFT_OUTPUT            a string
      SYFT_PARALLELISM       a string
      SYFT_ATTEST_PASSWORD   a string
      SYFT_ATTEST_KEY        a string
  Invocations that set `SYFT_ATTEST_PASSWORD` and `SYFT_ATTEST_KEY` still travel the
  same `packages` route (also combined with `-vv`/`-vvv` and each output format) and
  the same `version` route, and the callers read both output streams of those runs.

## Setup required
  - `/usr/local/bin/syft` must be the current build artifact of the `/app` module:
    after any change to the module, the binary must be rebuilt from `/app` and placed
    at that path (`go build -o /usr/local/bin/syft ./cmd/syft/`). The Go module cache
    is already populated by the image build (`go mod download`), so the rebuild needs
    no network access. The runtime is the image'"'"'s Go 1.19 Linux userland.
  - Callers construct the child environment from scratch; beyond `PATH` and `HOME` it
    contains only `SYFT_`-prefixed variables. No `-c/--config` flag is passed by any
    caller; cwd is `/tmp` and `HOME` is `/root`.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest runner, bootstrapped into a dedicated Python
  virtualenv by a shell script that installs the runner and then executes the test
  modules. Each contact with the implementation is one `subprocess.run` of the
  absolute binary path with an explicit env dict, cwd `/tmp`, captured stdout and
  stderr, and a 120-second per-invocation timeout; the JSON emitted on stdout is
  parsed on the calling side. The runner, the virtualenv, the bootstrap script, the
  env dicts, and the stdout parsing belong to the caller, not to the implementation;
  the engineer does not rebuild any of them.
