## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = cli (signals: ['"'"'build-artifact'"'"', '"'"'cli-invocation'"'"', '"'"'stdin-input'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-harness'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The SQLite command-line shell, invoked by absolute path:
      subprocess.run(['"'"'/app/sqlite3'"'"', db_file], stdin=open(sql_file, '"'"'r'"'"'),
                     capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
  /app/sqlite3 is the CLI binary produced by building the SQLite source tree
  that occupies /app itself: the upstream `sqlite` repository is cloned into
  /app at tag `version-3.30.1` (with `.git` removed) during the image build,
  then built with `./configure && make`. The source tree and the binary are
  project-owned. No system sqlite3 package is installed in the image and
  callers pass the absolute path, so nothing outside /app can answer these
  invocations. The image exports `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/app/.libs` and puts /app
  on `PATH`; callers use absolute paths and caller-created temp paths, so the
  working directory is not load-bearing.

## Must provide
  /app/sqlite3              executable CLI binary, rebuilt from the /app
                            source tree; accepts a single database-file path
                            argument and reads SQL statements from standard
                            input
  /app/.libs/libsqlite3.*   shared-library build artifacts under /app/.libs,
                            which the image'"'"'s LD_LIBRARY_PATH resolves at
                            runtime

## Called as
  sqlite3 <db-file>         db-file — argv[1]; a string holding a filesystem
                            path. Callers create the database file themselves
                            (initially zero bytes) before the invocation and
                            delete it afterwards. No other command-line
                            arguments are passed.
  standard input            a text SQL script redirected from a caller-written
                            temporary file: one or more semicolon-terminated
                            SQL statements per invocation. Statement kinds
                            callers use: CREATE TABLE, CREATE INDEX, CREATE
                            VIEW, INSERT, and SELECT queries using joins,
                            subqueries (including nested and VIEW-based right
                            sides), DISTINCT, WHERE, ORDER BY, the aggregate
                            functions COUNT/SUM/AVG/MIN/MAX, and the `||`
                            operator.

## Setup required
  - The source tree at /app must build with `./configure && make` (the build
    present in the image does so) and leave the `sqlite3` executable at
    /app/sqlite3; callers address that exact absolute path.
  - Runtime environment as set by the image: LD_LIBRARY_PATH includes
    /app/.libs and PATH includes /app.
  - No server or service state is involved; each invocation is a fresh
    process that opens the caller-created database file directly.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner (provisioned through `uv` by a shell runner script that
  changes into its own directory) drives the target through `subprocess.run`.
  The SQL scripts, the temporary `.sql` input files, the temporary `.db`
  database files, the per-invocation timeout, and the capture of
  stdout/stderr all belong to the calling side; they are created, supplied,
  and deleted by the caller and are not part of the implementation. The
  engineer must not rebuild any of it.
